{
  "id": "Compound::DB01072 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Atazanavir, a HIV protease inhibitor, is linked to Cyp3a4 through a series of molecular interactions that involve cytochrome P450 3a (CYP3A) enzymes. The evidence suggests that Atazanavir, as part of the CYP3A inhibitors class, binds to Cyp3a4, which is a key enzyme in drug metabolism. This relationship is supported by multiple paths, including direct interactions between Atazanavir and Cyp3a4, as well as indirect pathways through Darunavir and Lopinavir, both of which are also CYP3A inhibitors. The lowest common ancestors in these paths, such as Atazanavir and Darunavir, point to a shared molecular category—organonitrogen compounds—that may underpin the broader mechanism of action. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for Cyp3a4 in the metabolic clearance of Atazanavir and its related drugs, emphasizing the importance of CYP3A in drug metabolism and the interconnectedness of HIV protease inhibitors with this critical enzyme.",
